A symmetrical shape like a tent end or gable splits down its axis into two identical right-angled triangles, each holding **half** the span.
[tikz]\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\usetikzlibrary{calc,angles,quotes}
\coordinate (B) at (0,0);
\coordinate (C) at (6,0);
\coordinate (A) at (3,3.2);
\coordinate (M) at (3,0);
\draw (B) -- (C);
\draw (B) -- (A);
\draw (A) -- (C);
\draw[dashed] (A) -- (M) node[midway,right] {$h$};
\coordinate (ma) at ($(M)!0.3cm!(C)$);
\coordinate (mb) at ($(M)!0.3cm!(A)$);
\draw (ma) -- ($(ma)+(mb)-(M)$) -- (mb);
\pic[draw,"$\theta$",angle radius=0.7cm,angle eccentricity=2]{angle=C--B--A};
\node[below] at (1.5,-0.1) {$b/2$};
\node[below] at (4.5,-0.1) {$b/2$};
\node[above] at (A) {$A$};
\node[below left] at (B) {$B$};
\node[below right] at (C) {$C$};
\node[below] at (3,-0.1) {$M$};
\end{tikzpicture}[/tikz]

Two right-angled triangles side by side **share a side**, usually the vertical one; find that shared side first.
[tikz]\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\usetikzlibrary{calc,angles,quotes}
\coordinate (A) at (0,0);
\coordinate (B) at (7,0);
\coordinate (D) at (2.8,0);
\coordinate (C) at (2.8,2.6);
\draw (A) -- (B);
\draw (A) -- (C);
\draw (C) -- (B);
\draw (C) -- (D) node[midway,right] {$h$};
\coordinate (da) at ($(D)!0.3cm!(B)$);
\coordinate (db) at ($(D)!0.3cm!(C)$);
\draw (da) -- ($(da)+(db)-(D)$) -- (db);
\pic[draw,"$\alpha$",angle radius=0.8cm,angle eccentricity=1.9]{angle=B--A--C};
\pic[draw,"$\beta$",angle radius=0.8cm,angle eccentricity=1.9]{angle=C--B--A};
\node[above] at (C) {$C$};
\node[below left] at (A) {$A$};
\node[below right] at (B) {$B$};
\node[below] at (2.8,-0.1) {$D$};
\end{tikzpicture}[/tikz]
